(Colombo): Sri Lanka on Wednesday decided to cut off power supply to 10 hours a day across the country as hydropower could not work due to lack of hydropower in addition to the existing fuel shortage. This is according to the publication of CNA on Wednesday, March 30, 2022.
The Sri Lankan Electricity Authority said: "Electricity was cut off for 10 hours a day due to insufficient fuel to run the generator, while at the beginning of March there were only seven hours a day. " More than 40 percent of Sri Lanka's electricity is generated by hydropower, but most of the reservoirs are facing water shortages due to a lack of rainfall.
At the same time, most of the electricity generation is coal and fuel, but imports are also scarce because Sri Lanka does not have enough foreign currency to trade or pay for imports.
Fuel prices have soared since the beginning of the year as South Asia, with a population of 22 million, is in the worst recession since independence in 1948 due to a shortage of foreign currency. Imports.
