Another 19 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine, funded by the Chinese government and people, will arrive in Cambodia on Wednesday morning, March 30, 2022.
The vaccine to be delivered tomorrow is in the 5 million doses of the new 20 million doses that the Chinese government has announced to provide to Cambodia. About 1.5 million doses of the 5 million doses were delivered to Cambodia on March 29.
On the morning of March 29, Secretary of State for the Ministry of Health Yuk Sambath said that the Chinese aid vaccine would be used to boost the injection process. .
According to her, another 15 million doses of Chinese-assisted vaccines that have not yet been shipped are under discussion and could be shipped to Cambodia after September 2022. This is due to the fact that between April and September, Cambodia plans to receive up to 7 million doses of vaccines through the Kovak mechanism and other friendly countries.
It should be noted that 19 Kovid vaccines arrived in the country on the morning of March 29, about 1.5 million doses, and arrived on March 30, about 3.5 million doses, is the initial 5 million doses. The new 20 million doses that Chinese President Xi Jinping announced to Cambodia in a telephone conversation with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen on March 18.
China was the first country to provide 19 vaccines to Cambodia. The first batch of 600,000 doses of vaccines arrived in Cambodia on February 7, 2021.
To support Cambodia in the fight against Kovid 19 and to protect the lives of Cambodians from this global pandemic, China has provided a series of vaccinations. According to Ms. Youk Sambath, so far, China has provided a total of 28,724,800 doses of vaccine to Cambodia. Of that, 20 million doses is a new aid.
So far, Cambodia has vaccinated more than 92% of the population aged 3 years and older. Excellent results on the work of vaccination against Kovid 19, with Cambodia using the Chinese vaccine as a reference in its operations. In both orders and aid, Cambodia has received nearly 40 million doses of vaccines from China to serve in the vaccination campaign.
Japan to ban gold exports to Russia and may pass more sanctions
TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan will ban the export of precious metals, especially gold, to Russia in response to its invasion of Ukraine, the country's finance ministry said on Tuesday. According to Reuters.
The ban on exports of precious metals to Russia reflects Prime Minister Fumio Kishida's commitment to further sanctions against Russia, which he promised at last week's meeting of leaders from Russia. G7 advanced economies.
From April 5, Japan will ban the export of precious metals such as gold and other valuables, including luxury cars, jewelry, cosmetics and wine, Reuters reported.
Japan's move comes after the United States and Britain took steps to reduce gold trade with Russia.
Britain on Friday last week revised its sanctions guidelines to make it clear that British institutions could not help Moscow sell Russia's gold reserves, the US Treasury Department said in a statement. Earlier in the day, gold-related operations involving Russia could be sanctioned by US authorities.
Reuters reports, citing a Japanese government official, that gold exports to Russia will require prior approval by Japan's finance minister, as the new law will take effect on April 5. .
Kishida has announced plans to revoke Russia's most favorable trade status, which could lead to higher tariffs on Russian imports, as well as a ban on some imports from Russia.
Japan has also frozen the assets of more than 100 Russian individuals and institutions and is considering expanding financial regulations for Japan-based cryptocurrency exchanges. According to Reuters.
Thailand's tourism sector remains poor in first quarter 2022
BANGKOK: The Tourism Council of Thailand (TCT) says tourism conditions in Thailand in the first quarter of this year remained poor, while the risk of higher unemployment still dominated the outlook for the next quarter. Mostly caused by the omega virus and the war between Russia and Ukraine. According to the Bangkok Post.
According to a TCT survey of 200 international travelers between February 20 and March 10, 71% of respondents agreed that a quick test and PCR test on arrival To is a tedious process when visiting Thailand.
Meanwhile, TCT's tourism confidence index in the first quarter of this year stood at 44, a significant improvement from 36 in the same period in 2021, but lower than the 47-level level. In the fourth quarter last year.
The index reflects weak tourism confidence as the figure below 100 indicates low confidence among the tourism industry.
A survey of 704 operators between February 20 and March 10 also found that entertainment venues were most affected by pandemic prevention. The lowest confidence level was only 20, down from 41 in the same period last year.
Meanwhile, 14% of the 140 hotel owners in the nationwide survey continued to lay off staff, even though Thailand reopened in November last year. According to the Bangkok Post.
