(Phnom Penh): The spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police on March 26, 2022, completely rejected the content of the interview of Mr. Rath Rath Mony's wife on a foreign radio station, which was attacked in an arbitrary manner, inciting discrimination and Defamation affects the honor, prestige and dignity of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
A statement from the spokesperson of the National Police General Commission stated that on March 20, 2022, a foreign-language radio station in Khmer language broadcast an interview with a woman named Long Kim Heang who had been attacked. In a biased manner, inciting discrimination and slandering the truth, undermining the honor, prestige and dignity of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
"Therefore, the content of the interview of a woman named Long Kim Heang is an expression of anger, revenge, blindness, especially the words that are biased, narrow-minded, hateful and insulting to their country, which is an exaggeration, contrary to the truth, because if these statements are In fact, Long Kim Heang and her husband Rath Rath Mony could not be free to leave Cambodia. This is according to the spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police.
A spokesperson for the National Police General Commission also completely denied the content of the Khmer-language foreign radio interview with Long Kim Heang and described the interview as a forum set up for fraudulent purposes, creating confusion and incitement. Causes discrimination against the Royal Government of Cambodia for the purpose of political gain and instability in Cambodia.
Through the above explanation, the spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police hopes that the public as well as the national-international public opinion will consider all the reasons and judgments without any confusion and believe in the biased content that Long Kim Heang did not give an interview.
Cambodia praises China's leading role in providing and supplying millions of doses of Kovid-19 medicine to the Mekong countries
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia highly appreciates China's commitment to play a leading role in delivering millions of doses of medicines, medical supplies and Kovid-19 vaccine to the Mekong countries in a timely manner. This is according to the message of Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, congratulating the "Mekong-Lanchang Cooperation Week 2022" on March 26.
China is one of the 19 countries in the world that has produced 19 Kovid vaccines, including the World Health Organization (WHO) vaccine, and the vaccine. A report in late 2021 said that China had provided more than 1.5 billion doses of the vaccine to 106 countries and four international organizations, playing a key role in preventing the spread of the virus to developing countries.
For Cambodia, China's supply of medicines, medical supplies and Kovid-19 vaccine has contributed to the success of the national vaccination campaign, achieving a vaccination rate of 92% and reopening the country, he said. These achievements are a strong testament to the effectiveness and relevance of the Mekong-Lanchang Cooperation Framework.
To support Cambodia in the fight against Kovid 19 and to protect the lives of Cambodians from this global pandemic, China has provided ongoing assistance. According to Ms. Youk Sambath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, so far, China has provided a total of 28,724,800 doses of vaccine to Cambodia, while China has also announced an additional 20 million doses of vaccine.
US cancels talks with Taliban over reversal of girls' education pledges
Kabul: The United States immediately canceled a meeting with the Taliban in Doha, Qatar, scheduled to discuss major economic issues after the Taliban, now in control of Afghanistan, overturned its pledge. In order to allow girls to return to school. This was released by Reuters on Saturday, March 16, 2022.
A State Department spokesman said: "This decision is very disappointing and a reversal of their commitment to the people of Afghanistan as well as to the international community. "We have canceled a number of communications, including a planned meeting in Doha, and we have made it clear that we consider this decision to be a major turning point in our relations with the Taliban."
The Taliban on Wednesday reversed their promise to reopen schools for girls, saying they would remain closed until proper plans were made. Repeat. The decision has also drawn criticism from international humanitarian agencies and foreign governments.
