General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia trained standard operating procedures for investigating money laundering to more than 300 customs officials across the country
Speaking at the opening ceremony of the training, the Minister accompanied by the Prime Minister highlighted the role and responsibilities of the General Department of Customs and Excise in participating in the implementation of the anti-money laundering action plan set by the Joint Monitoring Team within the framework. International Cooperation (ICRG-JG) of the Finance Working Group (FATF).
In order to increase the efficiency of responding to this action plan, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia needs to establish standard operating procedures and conduct capacity building training for customs and excise officers who act as judicial police officers in investigating money laundering offenses. .
In the three training sessions, Mr. Se Sokhorn, Deputy Director General and Chairman of the Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorism Working Group, gave a presentation to all participants to understand three main topics, including: (1) general concepts of money laundering, (2) anti-money laundering progress, and (3) standard operating procedures for investigating money laundering offenses.
The Deputy Director-General explained the importance and background of standard operating procedures in accordance with the guidelines of the National Coordinating Committee against Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorism Financing and the Financing of Genocide, Customs Guidelines. World, Guidelines of the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime, and documents of the Finance Working Group.
Standard operating procedures are professional confidential internal rules that instruct customs and excise officials on how to work on identifying suspicious clues, concurrent investigations of criminal offenses, taking action on property and building criminal cases. Money sent to court.
At the end of each training session, all participants self-assessed their level of understanding of standard operating procedures, as well as provided feedback through an evaluation questionnaire.
The Cambodian Chamber of Commerce meets with the Construction and Real Estate Working Group to discuss private sector issues in the construction and real estate sector.
(Phnom Penh): The General Department of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce on March 24, 2022 organized a meeting of the "Construction and Real Estate" Working Group on the Private Sector of the Royal Government-Private Sector Forum to Discuss Challenges and Concerns Recently, the private sector in the construction and real estate sector at a time when Cambodia is facing the spread of Kovid-19 disease.
The purpose of this meeting is to report to the whole meeting on the past activities and achievements of the construction and real estate working group and to find new challenges, concerns and suggestions and submit to the whole meeting for consultation. Discuss the challenges of the private sector in the construction and real estate sectors as Cambodia faces the spread of Kovid-19 omega-C virus.
The purpose of this meeting is also to collect challenges, concerns, new requests from the private sector to submit a solution from a partner, the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction in the future.
Challenges that are of concern to members of the construction sector are current sales difficulties, ranging from the spread of new omega-3s and technical issues in the construction and real estate sectors.
