Thailand to ban use of digital currency for payments from April
BANGKOK, April 1 (Xinhua) - Thailand has issued a law banning the use of digital currency to pay for goods and services from April 1, the Thai market regulator said on Wednesday. According to Reuters.
Reuters quoted a statement issued by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) as saying that the move was in line with previous discussions between the SEC and the Bank of Thailand (BOT) on regulatory requirements. Made by digital asset business operators because it can affect the financial stability of Thailand and the economy as a whole.
The statement added that digital asset business operators providing such services must comply with the new law within 30 days of its entry into force.
The Central Bank of Thailand (BOT) has repeatedly said it does not support cryptocurrencies for payments, Reuters reported. The Fed will hold a press conference to discuss guidelines for banks' digital asset business.
In early January this year, regulators in Indonesia also warned financial companies not to provide and facilitate the sale of cryptocurrencies amid rising use of these currencies. According to Reuters.
Russian companies involved in Western sanctions are struggling to pay their debts (Video inside) (Moscow): Debt obligations of Russian companies linked to sanctioned Russian billionaires have made investors Concerns as interest payments (coupons) to foreign securities holders have been hampered by Western sanctions over Moscow's aggression on Ukraine.
The obstacles facing these companies underscore the huge impact of Western sanctions following Russia's invasion of Ukraine, as banks have been cautious for fear of violating sanctions.
Russia, which has called its attack on Ukraine a "special military operation", is also facing its own state payments for international investors. According to Reuters.
