Cambodian diplomat urges Myanmar stakeholders to exercise maximum restraint in using military force to defuse hostilities (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) He called on Myanmar to exercise maximum restraint in the use of military force to ease hostilities.
This was stated by the head of the Cambodian diplomatic corps at Phnom Penh International Airport this morning, March 23, 2022, during a press conference on his three-day working visit to Myanmar.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon's request was made during his first visit as Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair to Myanmar from March 21 to 23, 2022, in which he met with General Min Aung Lan. Chairman of the State Council of Myanmar as well.
Mr. Prak Sokhon stated, "With the expression of concern from members of the regional and international community on the ongoing violence, the declining human condition in Myanmar, I call for maximum tolerance for consumption. "The military is committed to easing hostilities, encouraging action to end violence and creating a conducive environment for constructive political dialogue to resolve the crisis."
According to Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, the situation in Myanmar has not yet been implemented, with a series of shootings and violence. Other political figures are not yet ready for reconciliation, as they still have ambitions and goals to defeat each other.
Mr. Prak Sokhon also requested the cooperation and support of the State Administrative Council for the successful organization of the Consultative Meeting on Humanitarian Aid, which is scheduled to be held in late April and early May.
"As we call for greater restraint, Min Aung Lan has raised concerns about the continued violence on the part of armed ethnic groups and the military," he said. "People's defense forces, as well as the smuggling of weapons, have led to serious violence."
The political crisis in Myanmar erupted over a dispute over the results of the November 8, 2020 election, which saw Aung San Suu Kyi's National League for Democracy (NLD) party win a landslide victory. To 83 percent or 396 of the total seats in the National Assembly.
However, Min Aung Lan accused the election of being rigged and warned that he would retaliate if the ballots were not recounted while Suu Kyi did not comply. Under the current constitution, Myanmar is governed by a power-sharing mechanism between civilian governments with the automatic retention of a 25 percent quota of parliamentary seats for the military.
According to Prak Sokhon, in Myanmar there are more than 120 ethnic groups and about 20 armed groups, while about 10 are all extremely strong. He urged Ming Ong Lang to continue to be patient for a conducive environment, reminding Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen that war could not be ended.
At the same time, he recalled Samdech Techo Hun Sen's visit to Myanmar in early January 2022, which paved the way for an appropriate solution for Myanmar to return to normalcy. During the visit, the Myanmar military regime decided to extend the ceasefire and allow humanitarian assistance, which are enshrined in the five-point unanimous agreement on a solution for Myanmar.
The five-point unanimous agreement on a solution for Myanmar was launched by the ASEAN Summit in April 2021 in response to the political crisis in Myanmar, including:
