A joint statement from the three ministries stated, "Fines not exceeding four million riels will be imposed on any gas station that violates Article 5 of this inter-ministerial law." "In the event that they have been fined once and continue to commit the same offenses, the retail station may be subject to a temporary shutdown."
Article 5 of this prakas states that all petrol retail stations must have regular gasoline and diesel products sold at prices not exceeding the set price. All petrol retail stations must have at least one type of regular gasoline (EA92) and one type of diesel for sale on a regular basis and are not allowed to sell only Super Petrol without the sale of diesel and regular gasoline. .
Officials from the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud of the Ministry of Commerce will monitor and fine in collaboration with the Ministry of Economy and Finance and the Ministry of Mines and Energy.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, once told Fresh News that the Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud Branch will continue to monitor this activity to take action against the station. And the Ministry's determination.
He stressed that all gas stations need to have regular stockpiles of gasoline and diesel for sale to the public, and not to sell only superfoods, and that sales must be in accordance with Ministry of Commerce regulations. At the same time, the Ministry of Commerce requires importers to have oil stocks for at least one month.
EU Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia to Participate in Humanitarian Mine Action in Cambodia
The Senior Minister briefed the President of the European Union Chamber of Commerce on the Royal Government of Cambodia's efforts and success in tackling landmines and explosive remnants in Cambodia over the past 30 years with the support of partners. Development and friendly countries, especially the achievement of complete peace throughout the country under the win-win policy of the late Prime Minister Samdech Techo.
At the same time, Cambodia has set a clear vision to end the landmine threat by 2025 and to continue to address the remaining issues of post-2025 post-war explosive remnants.
The Senior Minister added that in order to support the Royal Government's priority plan and achieve the vision of "Cambodia is mine-free by 2025", the Mine Authority has developed guidelines on the implementation of a demining mechanism that will ensure that all minefields Everything in the village has been cleared with the aim of speeding up demining and attracting the support of development partners and philanthropists.
Mr. Tassilo Brinzer expressed his condolences for Cambodia, which has been badly affected by the war and has left large quantities of landmines and unexploded ordnance across the country, endangering the Cambodian people.
At the same time, the President of the Chamber of Commerce praised and appreciated the efforts and achievements of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which has the Mine Authority as its staff in dealing with landmines and explosive remnants of war from the past.
