Major General Seng Thearin: Forces must be regularly trained to defend the motherland and the people
(Banteay Meanchey): Major General Seng Thearin, Deputy Commander of the 5th Army Division, said that the training work has to be done regularly and there is no end time, especially for young officers and soldiers. Retirement is only when you retire, if you continue to wear this uniform, continue this career, training work must be done regularly for the nation and the people.
Lt. Gen. Seng Thearin speaks at the opening ceremony of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces' (RCAF) Anti-Demonstration and Martial Arts Special Training Squadron at the 513th Infantry Battalion Headquarters on the morning of March 21, 2022.
Lt. Gen. Seng Thearin provided some important tips and advice, including: to pay attention to the rules and regulations, to have good solidarity with friends in the course by knowing how to help each other to participate As for helping the people affected by various natural disasters in a timely manner and maintaining the peace, political stability, security and social order that exist today at all costs.
At the same time, the Major General instructed all soldiers and trainees to maintain good hygiene and implement the measures of the Ministry of Health to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease.
It should be noted that the training course for infantry battalions lasted 61 days and the Special Forces against demonstrations and martial arts of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces lasted 134 days.
This afternoon, Samdech Techo Hun Sen announced the implementation of the National Program for Democratic Development at the Sub-National Level 2
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on Monday afternoon, March 21, 2022, will preside over the launching ceremony of the second phase of the National Program for Sub-National Democratic Development. 2021 to 2030, abbreviated as "KC II".
Decentralization and deconcentration reform is an important governance reform that focuses on the restructuring of sub-national public administration management systems based on key principles of good governance: accountability, transparency, efficiency, participation. Respond to the rule of law and the promotion of the environment and social equity.
The overarching goal of this reform is to create a culture of democracy at the grassroots level with the participation of the people, to improve the quality of public services and to develop the local economy that meets the real needs of the people in order to improve the quality of life of the people. And contribute to poverty reduction effectively.
The Sub-National Democratic Development Reform Program began at an historic early stage through the nationwide Commune / Sangkat Council elections and the introduction of a decentralized management system at the Commune / Sangkat level in 2002. Law on Commune / Sangkat Council Election and Law on Commune / Sangkat Administration.
In order to achieve the above vision, the NCDD must focus on strengthening and improving the structure, management system and functions of the sub-national administration, including the following:
* Improving the administrative capacity of sub-national administrations through strengthening and improving the management and development system of human resources in sub-national administrations, as well as the organization and implementation of information technology systems (digital governance).
* Sub-national administrations have more financial resources by increasing local revenue sources and transferring additional resources from the national level in a transparent, predictable manner and reducing the development gap between each sub-national administration. At the same time, sub-national administrations have more authority and responsibility in designing and implementing their plans and budgets.
Preparation, revision and strengthening of mechanisms, procedures and standards for the provision of public services and local economic development of sub-national administrations with efficiency, transparency, accountability, environment and social equity.
* Sub-national administrations can effectively assess, manage and respond to climate change vulnerabilities, disasters and epidemics to support the well-being of its people, sustainable development and local environment.
* Strengthening the authority of the Council of the Sub-National Administration to formulate policies, review and approve budget plans and other key priorities of the Sub-National Administration, and to address the proposals of Citizens to ensure the effectiveness of the sub-national administration.
The second phase of the National Program for Sub-National Democratic Development aims to "promote the provision of equitable and equitable democratic services and development through the modernization of the sub-national governance system and the improvement of "The process of providing services and quality of service to contribute to the eradication of poverty and improve the quality of life of the people."
This objective reflects and updates the objectives of the first NEC and the inclusion of strategic goals for governance of the Royal Government's Rectangular Strategy Phase 4, which emphasizes the modernization of the sub-national governance system in accordance with Rapid changes in Cambodian society and the needs of the nation in the future, especially as Cambodia becomes a high middle-income country by 2030.
K2 consists of the following five components:
Component 1: Leadership and Coordination of Reform: The expected achievement of this component is that national, sub-national coordination and support mechanisms will be strengthened to enhance the ownership and accountability of ministries and institutions. In designing and implementing sub-national democratic development reform programs effectively and efficiently.
Component 3: Human Resource Management and Development: The expected achievement of this component is that the sub-national administration has the right to choose, manage and use its staff based on the workload and financial capabilities of each sub-national administration and has Employees who have the will, loyalty and professional conscience in accordance with their roles and responsibilities, as well as regularly develop their abilities and professional skills.
Component 4: Decentralization of financial and planning and budgeting systems of sub-national administrations: The expected achievement of this component is that sub-national administrations have predictable budget resources and are appropriate to the functions and priorities of national policies and are appropriate. Allocated for flexible use in responding to the needs set out in the investment program development plan and strategic budget plan of the sub-national administration.
Component 5: Public Service Delivery and Local Development: The expected achievement of this component is that sub-national administrations have mechanisms, procedures and standards for effective public service delivery, local development and public communication, transparency, accountability, environment and Social equity through the implementation of general mandate or optional and mandatory functions to meet the needs of the people in its jurisdiction.
In addition to the above five components, the NCDD will also focus on two interdisciplinary tasks, including:
Promoting Gender Equality, Environment and Social Equity: This is the task of sub-national administration recruiting and managing staff, planning and resources, as well as providing non-discriminatory and non-discriminatory provision of public services and local development, and responding To vulnerable groups in a society with an environment and equity.
Management of Mitigation of Climate Change Vulnerability, Reduction of Risk of Disasters and Infectious Diseases: This task is that sub-national administrations can effectively assess, manage and respond to climate change vulnerabilities. Disasters and epidemics to support the well-being of the people and sustainable development in their localities.
Ministry of National Defense Hosts the 15th Annual Meeting of the ASEAN Institute of Security and Defense (NADI)
The two meetings will be chaired by Lt. Gen. Nem Sovath, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Politics and Foreign Affairs and Director of the Sena Techo Unit, with the participation of institutions and institutes of national defense and security of ASEAN member countries. A total of 13 institutions, including the Asian Vision Institute.
NADI is a network of research institutes of the 10 ASEAN member states, established in 2007 to bring together policy analysts specializing in national defense and security, scholars working on defense and security cooperation, and joint defense officials. Study and find solutions to the security challenges raised at the ASEAN Summit and the ASEAN Defense Ministers.
The Chairman of the Working Group of the Senior Minister of National Defense of Cambodia will also be invited to exchange views on the ASEAN military sector at the 15th Annual Meeting.
Among the many security mechanisms that have been put in place, NADI is one that has played a key role in bringing together research and perspectives for ASEAN defense institutions.
