Cambodian, Japanese PMs reaffirm their commitment to maintaining a world free of nuclear weapons (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Japanese Prime Minister Kishida Fermio March 2022 reaffirms commitment to achieving a world free of nuclear weapons.
The agreement between the Prime Ministers of the two countries was made during a bilateral meeting during the Japanese Prime Minister's two-day visit to Cambodia at the invitation of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
In a joint statement issued after the meeting between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Prime Minister of Japan, it was stated that "the two Prime Ministers reaffirmed their commitment to achieving a world without nuclear weapons. "
In addition, the two leaders shared the view that the Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT) is the cornerstone of the international denuclearization and non-proliferation system and that the two countries continue to work closely to Achieved meaningful results at the 10th NPT Conference in August.
In this regard, the Prime Minister reaffirmed Cambodia's strong support for Japan's initiative regarding the UN Resolution on "Joint Action and Future-Oriented Dialogue." "No Nuclear Weapons" adopted at the Plenary Session of the United Nations General Assembly on December 6, 2021.
The joint statement came after Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen held bilateral talks with his Japanese counterpart Kishida Fermi, who led a delegation to visit Cambodia.
A joint Cambodian-Japanese statement said: "The two prime ministers have called for an immediate end to the use of force and the withdrawal of military forces from Ukraine. "The two prime ministers stressed that the threat or use of weapons of mass destruction is unacceptable under any circumstances."
The two leaders shared the view that the invasion of a UN member state by violating the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the state is a serious violation of international law. The use of force is prohibited and is a serious violation of the UN Charter.
The two leaders acknowledged that the aggression was detrimental to the foundations of international order, which did not accept any unilateral modification of internationally recognized territories through the use of force. In the same spirit, the two Prime Ministers reaffirmed their commitment to maintaining peace, stability and security in the Indo-Pacific.
The two leaders also stressed that any armed attack on and threat against a nuclear plant established for peaceful purposes is a violation of international law.
In a press conference after today's meeting, Samdech Techo Prime Minister stressed from personal experience that "war can not end the war and should continue to find a peaceful solution."
"We both agreed that the Nay Pyi Taw authorities should implement the five-point unanimous agreement as soon as possible and facilitate the distribution of humanitarian aid to the people of Myanmar in need." This was confirmed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen in a joint press conference after the bilateral meeting.
During the talks between the Prime Ministers of Cambodia and Japan, the two leaders stressed the importance of actively implementing the distribution of humanitarian aid to the people of Myanmar in dire need, based on humanitarian principles, in coordination with the Center. Coordinate the ASEAN Humanitarian Aid and Disaster Management (AHA Center) and international organizations.
