Kishida Fumio had already visited Cambodia in 2014 when he was Japan's foreign minister.
Kishida Fumio's official visit to Cambodia underscores the two countries' strong commitment to promoting and strengthening existing good relations and strategic partnership, as well as cooperation between Cambodia and Japan. Bilateral and multilateral frameworks for the mutual benefit of both countries. This is the remark of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
With the visit of the Prime Minister of Japan to Cambodia, Fresh News would like to highlight some of the history of relations and cooperation that has emerged under the excellent relations between Cambodia and Japan:
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, a leader and a key figure in the improvement of Cambodia-Japan relations, has traveled to Japan most of the time, up to 23 times. There were four official visits in 2007, 2013, 2015 and 2015.
In addition to the visits mentioned above, many other senior Japanese government officials have also visited Cambodia.
Under this excellent relationship and cooperation, Cambodia and Japan have also established a lot of political cooperation. Cambodia has supported a number of Japanese policies, including: active contribution to peace, a free and open Indo-Pacific strategy, Japanese whaling, and a dialogue on the human rights situation in Cambodia.
Cambodia and Japan also have human rights cooperation and hold regular meetings on various issues to strengthen ties and cooperation.
Cambodia's exports to Japan include clothing, footwear, furniture, cosmetics, paper, stationery, leather, animals, metals, and more. In particular, the goods that Cambodia imports from Japan include: machinery, cars, meat, electrical appliances, furniture, paper, cotton, fabrics, all kinds of plastic products, sports equipment, all kinds of spare parts and so on.
As for investment, from 1994 to 2020, there are 144 Japanese investment projects in Cambodia with a total capital of nearly 3 billion US dollars.
Japan is also a country that has significantly cooperated with Cambodia in the fields of education, culture and health. According to the report, from 1992 to June 2021, the Japanese government provided scholarships to a total of 1,420 Cambodian students for long-term study in Japan.
Regarding the health sector, the Government of Japan is also a country that has supported and provided a lot of assistance to improve the health sector in Cambodia, especially during the Cambodia epidemic 19.
To support the fight against the Kovid 19 epidemic, Japan has provided more than $ 18 million for the purchase of medical equipment and ambulances, of which 100 have been donated to the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Japan has provided more than $ 6 million in emergency medical and technical assistance through the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime. Japan has also lent more than $ 400 million in emergency aid to respond to the Kovid 19 crisis.
Japan has also provided nearly $ 1 million in grants for temperature control and transportation equipment to support the Kovid 19 vaccination. Japan has also provided Cambodia with about 1.3 million doses of the vaccine.
According to the report, Japan is also a country that has provided substantial aid and loans to support development in Cambodia. From 1992 to 2021, the Japanese government provided a total of nearly $ 6 billion in aid and loans. Of that, nearly $ 4 billion in grants and nearly $ 2 million in loans.
Japan has also been active in supporting active demining in Cambodia. From 1994 to 2020, the Japanese government provided a total of $ 200 million in support for demining and UXO clearance activities.
In addition, Japan also provided support for the election and the Khmer Rouge tribunal.
Japan is also an important contributor to the employment of Cambodians to improve their lives and reduce poverty. From April 2019 to February 2022, Cambodia sent 687 first-class workers to work in Japan, 405 women. As of February 2022, there were 661 Cambodian workers and 387 women working in Japan.
According to the Japanese Embassy, as of January 2021, there were more than 4,000 Japanese nationals living in Cambodia and nearly 6,000 Cambodian students and citizens living in Japan. There are 693 scholarship students.
