(Phnom Penh): Research by a number of major institutions around the world working on public health issues has shown that the use of tobacco products poses a high risk to the health of consumers and second-hand smokers. Not only that, tobacco affects the lives of families and the national economy by millions of dollars every year.
What are the health effects of tobacco use? And what should stakeholders do to reduce smoking in Cambodia to a low level?
In the case of tobacco products, they refer to all kinds of products composed of tobacco sheets, tobacco fibers or tobacco substances produced by the tobacco industry or by other means used for smoking directly or in various forms. Others include: sucking, chewing, chewing or sniffing. This is according to Article 4 of the Law on the Control of Tobacco Products.
Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health, told the Cambodia-China Friendship Radio on March 17, 2022 via telegram that currently the prevalence of smokers in Cambodia is declining. The number of smokers is increasing as the population is increasing. Cigarette use causes various diseases that can lead to death:
Doctors say raising tobacco taxes is an effective way to educate people about smoking cessation, putting health warnings on cigarette packs, banning cigarette advertising on advertising wives and stopping sponsorship. Other under the embedded advertising content of the tobacco industry.
Increasing the tariff on tobacco products is the duty of the States Parties, meaning that Cambodia has ratified the States Parties to the Convention on Tobacco Control, so the States Parties must raise tariffs on tobacco products.
Research by the National University of Meanchey in 2021 shows that about 94 percent of Cambodians ask the state to increase taxes on tobacco products because the increase has no effect.
Are misconceptions related to the trafficking of illegal tobacco products and the loss of state tax revenue undermining the Royal Government's authority to raise tobacco taxes? Indeed, the tobacco industry often exaggerates its contribution and uses biased research under its auspices regarding the trafficking of illicit tobacco products to combat tobacco taxes and mislead policymakers and the public. .
It is estimated that about 11.6% of the global cigarette market is illegal, and most occur in low- and middle-income countries where cigarettes are cheaper. Scientific evidence shows that the market for illicit cigarettes in countries with low cigarette taxes and prices is higher than in countries with higher cigarette taxes and higher cigarette prices.
Currently, the tobacco tax in Cambodia is 25% of the retail price for domestically produced cigarettes and 31% for imported cigarettes, which is still low compared to other ASEAN countries' tobacco tariffs. The most reasonable tobacco tax rate recommended by the World Health Organization and the World Bank is 75% of the retail price.
Social observers see that most people mistakenly believe that smoking does not cost much and does not affect their health. But in fact, they spend a lot of money on cigarettes from day to day. If the average person smokes a pack of cigarettes a day, some of them can spend thousands of dollars a year, not to mention the cost of treatment for various diseases caused by tobacco use.
