Chhay Sivlin, president of the Cambodian Association of Travel Agents, told Fresh News that the lifting of restrictions would give Cambodia's tourism sector a chance to recover faster than expected.
She said that in the past, even though Cambodia has been open for many months, there are still restrictions on documents and PCR testing, which is a barrier for guests who want to visit Cambodia.
She noted that since Cambodia reopened the country and managed the Kovid 19 infection well, international visitors, mostly from Europe, Indonesia, Singapore, Malaysia and Thailand, have steadily increased, but not by much. Due to transportation issues and various barriers.
Despite the abolition of this condition, the Ministry of Health still encourages all passengers to self-test. In addition, all passengers must present a Kovid 19 vaccination card upon arrival in Cambodia. Passengers who are not fully vaccinated will be required to undergo a 14-day trial.
Thuon Sinan, president of the Asia-Pacific Tourism Association of Cambodia, also told Fresh News that some of the current conditions for travelers are a source of hope for Cambodia's tourism sector, which he hopes will boost tourism growth. More tourists.
He pointed out that in the past, most visitors to Cambodia, especially Europeans, came from ASEAN, not their home countries.
He said that what helps to promote the recovery of tourism is all ASEAN countries together. If ASEAN does not have a common policy, it will not be able to help restore tourism, as the Russia-Ukraine war crisis has become a new obstacle for tourists in the world, which hinders the recovery. Of tourism both in Cambodia and in the whole world.
He said that if the war between the two countries continues and gas prices continue to rise, it will affect the travel of international visitors and even national tourists may reduce travel. For the affluent, it is still traveling, but for the middle and lower middle class, there may be a significant decrease due to the continuous increase in gas prices.
Ms. Chhay Sivlin expressed concern that the continuous increase in gasoline prices will affect the arrangement of tour packages for both domestic and foreign guests, as the price of these packages will increase unexpectedly and will affect the mood of guests.
It should be informed that last weekend, Cambodia received more than 250,000 guests, an increase of 27.30% compared to last weekend, including more than 240,000 national guests and more than 10,000 domestic guests.
