The IMF posted on its website that in addition to affecting human lives and the historic influx of refugees, the war was raising food and energy prices, boosting inflation and hurting incomes while hurting trade. Supply chain and foreign deposits in some of Ukraine's neighboring countries.
The IMF said the war also hurt business confidence and increased uncertainty among investors, which would hurt asset prices, tighten financial conditions and could spur capital outflows from developing countries.
Citing the growing risk of unrest in a number of regions, from sub-Saharan Africa and Latin America to the Black Sea and Central Asia, the IMF said countries with direct trade, tourism and financial risks The object will be under greater pressure.
At the same time, food insecurity is likely to increase in parts of Africa and the Middle East, where Egypt imports 80 percent of its wheat from Russia and Ukraine.
In the long run, the IMF said, "this war could change the basis of economic order and political geography if energy trade changes supply chains, restructures the distribution network and countries reconsider holding foreign exchange reserves." According to Reuters.
The war in Ukraine could push some poor countries to face grain shortages
The World Bank (WB) says some developing countries are facing short-term wheat shortages due to their heavy reliance on Ukraine's wheat exports, which have been disrupted by the World Bank. Russian aggression. According to Reuters.
The World Bank said in its latest Trade Monitoring Report that Gambia, Lebanon, Moldova, Djibouti, Libya, Tunisia and Pakistan were most at risk of disrupting Ukraine's wheat exports. About 40 percent or more of those countries' wheat imports.
"Those importing countries will find it difficult to switch to alternative sources of supply, which could lead to short-term supply shortages," the World Bank said.
Grain supply is worsening due to Russia's restrictions on wheat and other grain exports to countries except members of the Asia-Europe Economic Union (EEU), including Armenia and Belarus. Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan.
Russia is the world's largest exporter of wheat in 2018, and Ukraine is the fifth largest exporter of wheat, according to World Bank data. The two countries together account for about a quarter of global wheat exports. According to Reuters.
