Minister of Commerce: RCEP agreement is a long-term intervention for Cambodia to end its status as a least developed country by 2028
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, stated that the implementation of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) is a long-term intervention for Cambodia to end its status as a least developed country this year. 2028 and the planned efforts to achieve high middle income status by 2030 and high income by 2050.
Mr. Pan Sosak further stated that for Cambodia, this agreement provides a modern legal platform that encourages the country to establish more liberal agreements, using the ASP agreement as a model or limit of liberalization, and to carry out various reforms to ensure Its standards, which may be proportional to the extent to which this Agreement is acceptable, including in the areas of infrastructure, law and institutions.
The Minister of Commerce also stated that according to a study by the Economic Research Institute for ASEAN and East Asia (ERIA), Cambodia is ready to achieve a 7.3% increase in exports, 23.4% in Investment and 2% of GDP from the implementation of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP).
He added that in addition to the RCEP, Cambodia also creates other positive effects, including the opportunity to rehabilitate Kovid-19 epidemic and post-epidemic structural development through job opportunities. Promoting investment, reducing poverty and promoting environmental benefits from this agreement.
