(Stung Treng): Speaking during the inspection of Kovid 19 vaccination in Stung Treng province, Mrs. O Vandin, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health and Chair of the Kovid 19 Vaccination Committee, called on all people to be careful about avian influenza. A new type of Influenze A / H9 after finding a case in children in Chi Kreng district, Siem Reap province.
In an interview with reporters, Ms. O Vandin claimed that this type of bird flu is very rare, but although it is rare, people must be extremely careful. She added that the bird flu was no different from Kovid 19, meaning that the symptoms at the time of infection were almost identical to those of Kovid 19.
To prevent and prevent the spread of bird flu, Ms. O Vandin warned people to avoid contact with all kinds of birds and do not go to poultry farms. In particular, traders selling or raising birds must use proper means of self-protection by wearing masks and gloves when in contact, and must wash their hands with soap after touching birds.
The health official added that when eating other foods must be finely cooked to avoid eating meat that is not cooked well and not cleaned properly. Separately, vegetables and fruits should be washed and placed separately with uncooked meat. On the other hand, the knife must be cleaned thoroughly, avoiding just wiping and not washing with soap and water.
The ministry confirmed that there have been dead chickens in the affected villages since February. Immediately after this case, the Ministry sent officers from the Department of Infectious Diseases to cooperate with the provincial emergency response team to investigate, prevent and prevent epidemics and health education to local people.
Listen to O Vandin's full statement below: Join the survey: The National Center for Commercial Arbitration and the European Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia jointly conduct a survey on the need for trade dispute resolution Extracurricular Services
(Phnom Penh): A strong trade dispute resolution mechanism is very important for the business community. Surveying is an important point to assess the investment climate in the market in order to develop effective non-judicial dispute resolution for commercial disputes.
We have collaborated to conduct this survey to achieve the following objectives:
* First, to measure the level of understanding, use and market demand for the settlement of extrajudicial trade disputes through arbitration and conciliation.
* Second: To measure the use and satisfaction of existing mechanisms for out-of-court dispute resolution.
* Third: To receive feedback to improve awareness, strengthen services and meet user expectations.
This form of feedback is useful in boosting the economy, especially in creating a conducive environment for the business community. The reason is that understanding relationships builds good relationships and prevents conflicts. In the event of a dispute, mediation helps maintain the relationship. Reconciliation helps the parties to explore and make their own choices and be flexible in the process.
The survey will take about 20 minutes to complete. We truly appreciate your time. You can continue to share this survey with other stakeholders. Deadline for responses is April 7, 2022.
Book authors inspire young people to value reading
(Phnom Penh): A group of book authors, including monks, business owners, public and private employees, as well as students, came together to share their book writing experiences, personal experiences and the benefits of reading.
A friendly gathering held last weekend on the occasion of the 7th National Reading Day took place at River House Veal Sbov, co-organized by the author of No One is Born Poor, Pannasastra University of Cambodia, private companies and youth groups. Volunteering for social development The main purpose of this gathering is to promote the spirit and value of reading books.
On that occasion, Mr. Veng Sereyvuth, the author of the book, no one is born poor after sharing real life experiences and highlighting the benefits of reading: "Books can reflect, enable us to be prepared, can provide intellectual methods "High and allows us to anticipate challenges."
At the same time, he also encourages people to read more of their favorite books. Reading books helps us to correct bad habits, inactivity, and progress.
Professor Sambo Manara, a professor of Khmerology and Khmer History at Pannasastra University of Cambodia, said that writing a book takes more or less time, depending on the needs of the writer-reader relationship.
"The writer's vision certainly looks farther than you can, but when it comes to writing time, there are limits, dates, and responsibilities," he said.
It should be informed that on March 11, Cambodia celebrated the 7th National Reading Day under the theme "Reading helps improve the quality of life in the digital society".
The Ministry of Education organizes this day every year in order to commemorate the heroism and noble merits of Samdech Preah Sangkhareach Chuon Nath, who worked hard to protect the heritage of Khmer literary culture and in order to cultivate reading habits, promote reading culture, enhance capacity. Reading, composing, and researching to contribute to the protection and promotion of Khmer culture, civilization, and knowledge to all Cambodians.
Tbong Khmum Governor Wants to Improve Doeum Po Tum Teav Historic Site as a Historic Site to Attract Tourists
(Tbong Khmum): Dr. Cheam Chansophoan, Governor of Tbong Khmum Province and Ms. Meng Van Danin, led a professional officer to inspect and study the historical site of Deum Po Tum Teav in Cheung Kal Village, Mong Rieu Commune, Tbong Khmum District, Tbong Khmum Province.
With this inspection, the provincial governor also instructed all professional officials to study and organize this historical site to become a historical tourist site for the people to visit and study the history of both national and international guests. He said the first step was to study the construction of a fence and plant more saplings on the site of the historic Po tree, including the provision of toilets.
Please be informed that recently, a Po tree in a large historical area called Tum Teav, called Po Neak Ta Krahorm, was blown down by the wind and re-planted by the provincial authorities. After the fall of the big Po tree, the historical area of Tum Teav was interesting to see and know by the people by visiting, especially people from other provinces as well.
Senior Civil Aviation official: Japan is considering reconnecting direct flights with Cambodia soon
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sin Chanserivutha, Undersecretary of State and Spokesperson for Civil Aviation, confirmed that due to the crisis of Kovid 19 epidemic, the flight connection between Cambodia and Japan has not yet been completed. However, the Japanese side is considering re-connecting flights with Cambodia soon.
The spokesman said the resumption of flights was due to declining market and demand for air travel, while measures by Japan were still tight, making it difficult for people to travel. Travel in and out.
He said that now, if Cambodians or Japanese people want to travel between Cambodia and Japan, they have to go through other countries such as Thailand, Singapore and Korea before entering Cambodia or Japan.
Flights have played an important role in boosting economic activity, diplomacy, tourism and investment. Japan is one of the potential investors in Cambodia among other countries.
As of the end of October 2021, the Council for the Development of Cambodia has welcomed 146 Japanese investment projects with a total investment capital of approximately 2,853 million US dollars, of which 66 investment projects are located in special economic zones with a total investment capital. About 340 million US dollars.
Russia denies threatening to seize assets of Western companies (Video inside) (Moscow): The Russian Embassy in the United States has denied reports that Russian prosecutors have threatened to seize and seize the assets of Western companies. The West, because it is not true. This is according to the publication of the Russian newspaper RT on Monday, March 14, 2022.
The Russian embassy in the United States has denied the allegations, saying the Wall Street Journal article was a hoax. The embassy also urged local media to stop publishing fake news, claiming that "despite the most difficult circumstances, US trade interests in the Russian market have not been violated in any way."
