In a series of statements issued by the Chinese Foreign Ministry, Beijing did not support Russia's war in Ukraine, nor did it condemn Russia. So what is China thinking and where is China standing for sure?
The New York Times, based on sources close to US officials, said in early March that China was aware of Putin's military operations. In early February, senior Russian officials postponed an attack on Ukraine until the end of the Beijing Winter Olympics. But China has denied such reports.
Through a number of Chinese Foreign Ministry statements, Beijing has expressed a "neutral stance" on the basis of China's foreign policy: non-interference in the internal affairs and respect for the territorial integrity of other countries, including Ukraine. Well.
According to experts, China does not want to see the war in Ukraine erupt because Russia is a close friend and Ukraine is a business partner that has participated in Xi Jinping's Belt and Road Initiative (BRI) since 2017. Come on. On the other hand, China does not like unrest because the Chinese economy and the global economy are interconnected and what China wants is stable trade for its economic growth.
So can China benefit from this war? According to Chinese intellectuals and state media, in the Ukraine war, at least it made the United States and Europe join hands with Russia, especially as it could distract the United States from the Indo-Pacific strategy or monitoring activities. China in Asia for a while, and even better, this war will make Russia more dependent on China.
China is known as one of the most cautious countries and a new superpower emerging on the international stage until the United States is considered its greatest geopolitical rival in the 21st century. This. Relations between Russia and China have grown steadily from year to year. But China will not provide military assistance or a military solution to Russia.
The United States and NATO have so far insisted they will not send troops to help Ukraine fight Russia, but have warned that they are ready to take immediate action if Russia provokes them. Threatening or launching any attack on NATO member countries.
Is China-Russia relations an allied system?
For China, Putin's war in Ukraine could clearly show that China was right, because if relations between China and Russia were allies, China would have to defend or join the war with Russia. Non-alignment has long been a Chinese policy, with Chinese intellectuals agreeing that outside the League, China is free to pursue its own foreign policy.
