Draft Cambodian Cashew Policy Reaches Final Income Collection as Prices and Yields Fall This Year
(Phnom Penh): The draft national policy of Cambodian cashew has reached the final input to be submitted to the upcoming cabinet meeting as the price and yield of cashew this year is declining, which makes farmers face Losses as oil and fertilizer prices soared.
Reach Ra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce and Deputy Chairman of the Cambodian Cashew Policy Joint Working Group, told Fresh News on March 11 that the Ministry of Commerce is currently collecting the final input from the Cambodian Cashew Policy. Relevant to be completed soon and submitted to the Cabinet meeting.
He said the Ministry of Commerce wanted the policy to be released earlier this year to boost cashew prices, as well as increase processing and exports.
He noted that at present, Cambodian cashews are facing some challenges: the cultivation of farmers is not in line with the technology, the cultivation is not yet resistant to the weather, the use of drugs and fertilizers are not up to standard, especially the prevention of pests to destroy and not. There are still many processing factories in Cambodia.
He claimed that when the Cambodian cashew policy is officially released, it will help solve these problems, especially to attract more processing factories, because the quality of Cambodian cashews and the international market are in demand, while Cambodia has investment laws that are favorable to investors. From abroad.
He added: "We now have a number of processing plants, but we want more to process, avoid we collect, and we export unprocessed products to neighboring and international markets, which makes us lose value and trade. "And our processors still lack the capital to stockpile."
He said it rained in mid-February when the cashews were blooming and the sky was overcast for days without damaging the flowers and petals. This change in weather has caused the quality of grains to fall sharply, causing traders to lower the price of cashews.
He said that to ensure the stability of cashew prices, Cambodia needs at least 50 to 100 processing plants, while currently Cambodia has only four large processing plants. He expects that Cambodia's cashew policy will be issued soon to attract more foreign investors to build factories in Cambodia to ensure the stability of cashew prices to farmers. Cambodia
(Oddar Meanchey): Mr. Khin Nhean, Vice Chairman of the Cambodian Red Cross Branch Committee of Oddar Meanchey Province, on behalf of Mr. Pen Kosal, Chairman of the Provincial Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross, this morning, March 11, 2022, led the Cambodian Red Cross Humanitarian Donation Team. Distributed to 54 families affected by strong winds, causing damage to houses in Anlong Veng and Trapeang Prasat districts, Oddar Meanchey province.
