The Cambodian representative made the remarks in response to the US delegation's attack on human rights and political parties at the General Assembly on the second agenda of the 49th session of the UN Human Rights Council last week. March 08, 2022.
The Cambodian representative said that the United States' use of human rights issues as a weapon of attack to serve the political agenda was well known to many delegates to the Supreme Human Rights Council. While calling on the above delegation to evaluate the information thoroughly and not to politicize, he would like to take this opportunity to raise some facts about Cambodia as follows:
First, the statement that the opposition was the target of the attack is not true at all. Joining any political party or civil society organization is not a license to violate the law with impunity. The accused person is on the basis of the offense determined by law, not because of who that person is. Many examples show that individuals who support the ruling party in cases of violation of the law are also convicted by the same Cambodian court.
Third, due to time constraints, we ask the US delegation to understand our earlier statement regarding the question of peaceful assembly and the right to strike.
"Advocacy for human rights needs to start in our own country," said An Sokhoeun. "Unfortunately, this advocacy country has been facing systemic challenges in the field of human rights for many years, including double standards and hypocrisy."
According to the Cambodian representative, in 2021, more than 700 dissidents in the United States were arrested and charged with trespassing after storming a parliament building. In the same year, more than 50 journalists were arrested and detained throughout the United States. Opposition presidents' social media accounts have also been banned. The list of human rights issues is not exhaustive.
Cambodia's Permanent Representative to the United Nations urges US delegation to heed UN Secretary-General António Guterres' call for "human rights must not be used as a weapon of mass destruction" Other countries by two standards "If we want to maintain the highest integrity and credibility of the Human Rights Council.
