To reduce the confusion of this information, professional journalists have also begun to verify some of the information with those involved in the story, claiming that some of the information was falsified or exaggerated or unintentional. By continuing to share without any consideration or verification. Here are some examples that have been found to be false:
1: Actor in the war in Ukraine:
A video of a reporter in front of a crowd packed in black plastic bags has been posted on several major social media sites and has been widely shared by Kremlin supporters. In fact, the video was taken during a climate change protest in Vienna, Austria, in early February.
2: The wife of the Vice President of Ukraine joined the fight with the army?
Social media users have shared a picture showing the wife of the Ukrainian vice president joining his country's armed forces to fight Russian aggression. Investigators claim the photo was taken by a Ukrainian female soldier during rehearsals for a military parade in Kyiv in August 2021. The Ukrainian government does not have a vice president.
A video clip posted showing fighter jets and bombers flying over a city with the sound of an air strike siren has caused a stir on social media. A BBC review found that the video was in preparation for a military parade in 2020.
6: Ukraine staged a war from Russia?
A video of a young woman and a young man with blood on their faces has garnered millions of views on social media, which is supposed to be a scene hired to perform about the attack. From Russia. But in fact, the video has nothing to do with the war in Ukraine, it was filmed in 2020 for a TV series in Ukraine and was released to the public in December of that year.
Social media users can limit the spread of false information by taking a few minutes before sharing to consider whether what they are seeing or reading can be true and from a reliable source. . Verification with more reliable sources can help prevent or reduce this false information and images from being shared more and more.
