Financial knowledge and means for women and women entrepreneurs are fully supported by public and private institutions
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Women's Affairs, National Bank of Cambodia and Visa have completed their one-year project on "Promoting Financial Literacy for Women and Women Entrepreneurs", contributing to the promotion of financial literacy. Object and increase economic empowerment for women, entrepreneurs, women and nearly 10,000 students across Cambodia.
The project begins with training sessions in target provinces including Siem Reap, Kratie, Stung Treng and Preah Vihear for participants from the Ministry of Women's Affairs, relevant departments and the Women's Development Center on digital finance knowledge and key financial skills.
Through four discussion forums, the project shares the knowledge and financial means necessary for making the right financial decisions, promoting entrepreneurship and continuing to grow.
Mr. Thai Chhun Hak, Director General of Gender Equality and Economic Development of the Ministry of Women's Affairs, said that Cambodian women are a treasure trove of human resources with high talent, skills and creativity, and we hope that through this project they can use the knowledge gained. To develop themselves, leaving life at the micro, economic and informal level.
He added that they also hope that they can share their new knowledge and success to other women by disseminating financial knowledge and financial environment for the benefit of the people. The whole of Cambodia.
The financial environment remains a source of social and economic inequality in Cambodia, especially for Cambodian women. This is not only the result of unequal access to financial services, but also access to credit and deposit mobilization, which shows a large gender gap, meaning that women have less savings and are able to afford it. Get a smaller loan compared to male clients.
Mr. Rath Sovannarak, Director General of the National Bank of Cambodia, said that the National Bank of Cambodia has identified financial literacy as an important pillar in promoting the digital economy and promoting equal access to financial services and accessibility. Be an entrepreneur. We are pleased to partner with Visa to improve the financial environment in Cambodia.
Ms. Chum Monica, Managing Director of Visa Cambodia, said that Visa is pleased to partner with the National Bank of Cambodia and the Ministry of Women's Affairs to implement projects that support the financial development of women in Cambodia. Visa believes that an inclusive economy in all areas will contribute to development everywhere.
