Shares in Cambodia start to recover after falling during the outbreak of war between Russia and Ukraine (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Hong Sohour, Royal Government Delegation in charge of the General Manager of the Cambodia Securities Exchange (CSX) said. That the share price in Cambodia has started to rise again after falling during the beginning of the war between Russia and Ukraine.
Speaking to Fresh News by telephone on March 8, Hong Sohour said the decline could be due to global events, local events and the company's own situation.
He said the effects of the war between Russia and Ukraine, which was heating up, did not seem to have significantly changed stock market activity.
He said that as the war between the two countries erupted, there were some signs of volatility, leading to a sell-off that caused a large number of stocks to fall. But after a while, the acquisition activity started to pick up and the stock price continued to rise to this day.
"As soon as that happened, it went down a lot and several days later it started to come back up slowly until today it didn't go down," he explained. "When an event happens, it can affect, it can go down or up, but after a while, investors start to incorporate information into their decisions, so he has to adjust to the event."
On February 24, during the start of the Russian-Ukrainian war, the first day, the share price of some companies fell to 600 riel per share. And on March 7, 2022, the stock price began to rise slightly.
Currently, there are 17 companies listed on the stock exchange, including 9 equity companies and 8 bond companies.
In 2022, CSX expects seven more companies to enter the stock market. However, Hong Sohua worries that the two crises, the outbreak of the omega virus and the Russia-Ukraine war, could indirectly affect their readiness to enter the stock market. Spokesman for the General Commission: 743 more vehicles were inspected and fined on March 08, 2022
