Oil rises to record high since 2008 as US, Europe consider buying off Russian oil (Video inside) (Singapore): Oil prices on Monday hit a record high since 2008. The United States and its European Union are considering a ban on Russian oil purchases, while delaying the return of Iranian crude to world markets has fueled supply concerns. According to Reuters.
Before falling back to the above level in the first few minutes of trading on Monday morning, the two crude price indices rose more than $ 10 a barrel to a high. The highest was in July 2008, when Brent crude traded at $ 139.33, while WTI traded at $ 130.5 a barrel.
US Secretary of State Antony Blinken has said the United States and Europe are looking into ways to ban Russian oil imports, and the White House has coordinated with key congressional committees to work on the commission's own bans.
As for the talks on the Iran nuclear deal, the talks were in limbo last Sunday after Russia demanded that the United States ensure that the sanctions it faced on the Ukraine conflict would not affect its trade with Iran. . China has also raised new demands.
In response to Russia's demands, US Secretary of State Antony Blinken said on Sunday that the sanctions imposed on Russia over its invasion of Ukraine had nothing to do with the Iran nuclear deal. According to Reuters.
