General knowledge: 10 oldest countries
(World): Although human life began to take shape millions of years ago, the latest signs of human civilization have only recently emerged. Some civilizations developed during about 6500 BC, when people stopped living as nomads and began to settle and develop in certain areas. These new settlements gave rise to many cities and subsequent states.
Here are the 10 oldest countries in the world that still have territory to this day, not the former states that have lost territory. These countries include: 1: Egypt 6000 BC
It was not until the 1960s and 1970s that a site was found that provided clear evidence for the existence of the Xia Dynasty, as many believed it to be more of a fairy tale than a fact.
Around 66 BC, the Roman Empire and the Persian Empire (present-day Iran) fought over present-day Georgia for more than 700 years to occupy the territory.
The ancient times of Greece were one of the most prominent periods known as the foundations of modern Western civilization. The glorious period of Greek history began in 800 BC, after Greece began to emerge from the Dark Ages.
Although the Japanese emperor Jimmu is almost a fairy tale, Japan celebrated its National Day on February 11 in gratitude to the emperor who ruled Japan in 660 BC. 9: Iran 550 BC
