International Energy Agency member states decide to release more than 60 million barrels of oil
The International Energy Agency (IEA) says its member states have decided to release more than 60 barrels of oil to offset rising prices due to Russia's invasion of Ukraine. According to Reuters.
Of the 61.7 million barrels of reserves released, the United States will release 30 million barrels, Japan 7.5 million barrels, South Korea 4.4 barrels and Germany 3.2 million barrels, according to Reuters. Barrel.
The decision was made to compensate for the supply disruption, as sanctions on certain parts of Russia's economy forced many buyers to avoid buying Russian oil.
The tense oil market was not affected by the news and instead rose sharply after the announcement of the release on Tuesday and rose to a record high on Thursday. Nearly $ 120 per barrel.
"In the last few days since the decision, each member of the IEA has been considering whether to contribute," the Paris-based agency said in a statement. "How much oil will be released to the announced response plan according to their local circumstances?"
The IEA is one of the 31 largest industrialized nations in the world, but not Russia, and says it is open to releasing more oil if needed. According to Reuters.
