Agricultural drones spraying pesticides to bring to Kampong Speu reduce human labor
(Kampong Speu): A trial on a 135-hectare plantation filled with mango, lime and lemon crops by 39-year-old Hean Vannarin, a farmer in Kampot, Tasal commune, Oral district, Kampong Speu province, was conducted successfully. Fertilizer protects flowers and fruits through the use of agricultural drones.
Mr. Hean Vannarin said that in the past he usually had to pay at least 50 workers to take care of the crops on the plantation, but now, when the industry is booming, people are turning to factory workers. On the other hand, the agricultural sector is facing a crisis due to lack of labor, and when there are agricultural drones in the territory of Kampong Speu can reduce the use of many people.
Mr. Toch Nhav, Director of the Mechanical Office of Kampong Speu Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that most of the land in Kampong Speu is now cultivated with agricultural crops and the other part is factories and enterprises. Traditionally, manpower is used as a means, but as technology becomes more sophisticated, farmers in developed countries are using machines instead of manpower.
He said this was the first time that farmers in the Oral district had used agricultural drones instead of manpower to spray pesticides and care on leaves and other pesticides.
He added that he wants all farmers to use modern technology instead of manpower, like some developed countries that have the same agricultural sector, with agricultural drones really replacing a lot of manpower on shooting. Kill insects that give back time, money, labor, especially without affecting health.
Agricultural drones can work up to 16 hectares per hour with only two managers, unlike traditional firing, which requires many people to carry buckets and spray them on crops, facing direct exposure to chemicals. . Low labor costs can give you the opportunity to do other work.
