The Embassy of Japan in Cambodia organized a farewell ceremony for 36 scholarship students before going to study in Japan
(Phnom Penh): The Embassy of Japan in Cambodia on the afternoon of March 3, 2022 organized a farewell ceremony for 36 Cambodian students who received scholarships from the Government of Japan.
The farewell ceremony was presided over by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia, Mr. Youk Ngoy, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Mr. Ngov Peng Huy, President of the Cambodian Alumni Association from Japan at the Multipurpose Hall of the Embassy of Japan.
Speaking at the event, Mr. Mikami Masahiro congratulated the 36 students who received Japanese Government Scholarships after going through a rigorous selection process and thanked the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports for their excellent cooperation. From the beginning to the final selection stage.
Despite the unpredictable situation of Kovid 19, the measures for travel to Japan are gradually easing, the Japanese ambassador said. In this case, Japanese Government Scholarship (MEXT) students are given priority in traveling to Japan. Therefore, he hopes that all scholarship students will travel to study in Japan as expected.
On that occasion, Mr. Mikami Masahiro expressed his belief that all Japanese Government Scholarship students will continue to contribute to the development of Cambodia in various fields after graduation and return home. At the same time, he also hopes that while you are studying in Japan, you will enjoy exploring and gaining experience related to Japanese culture, as well as making many friends with Japanese and other peoples around the world. .
"Next year is the year of the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Japan and Cambodia," he said. The friendly relations between our two countries have been closely linked due to the support from Japanese government scholarship alumni in many ways. In addition, he hopes that you will continue to follow the older students to continue to strengthen the relationship between our two countries in the future.
