Japanese Government Provides Over $ 260,000 Grant for Small-Scale Human Security Project in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through its Embassy in Cambodia, has provided $ 264,086 to three entities under the Small Grants for Human Security Project (Kusanoni Grant).
The signing ceremony was held at the Embassy of Japan under the presidency of Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan and the three recipients on the morning of March 4, 2022.
* First: Project to install satellite prosthesis and physical therapy at Kratie Provincial Rehabilitation Center in Kratie Province (US $ 92,390) Recipient Unit: Kratie Provincial Rehabilitation Center
The grant will be used to install 15 types of equipment for the production of prostheses and satellites and seven other types of equipment for physical therapy at the Kratie Provincial Rehabilitation Center (PRC) in Kratie. The PRC has been facing a number of problems due to the deterioration of equipment needed for the production of prostheses and satellites, as well as the lack of equipment for rehabilitation activities.
Through this project, it is expected that about 500 patients who come to the PRC each year will benefit from improved equipment and quality of prostheses and satellites.
In this project, the grant will be used for the reconstruction of old small water treatment plants and the installation of necessary equipment in eight communes in Banteay Meanchey, Battambang, Siem Reap and Prey Veng provinces. Water Supply 1001 has been contributing to improving access to completely safe drinking water in rural Cambodia through the installation of water filters.
Through this project, more than 78,000 people in the target areas will have better access to safe and affordable drinking water.
* 3rd: Water supply project at Kanan Hope School in Kampong Chhnang Province (US $ 88,440) Recipient Unit: Kanan Hope School
In this project, four atmospheric water generators and one lithium battery will be installed at Kanan Hope School in Kampong Chhnang province. At this school, many students find it difficult to get safe drinking water because the well water they regularly use is dirty.
Through this project, it is expected that 560 students and 60 teachers will have access to clean water. In addition, the project can provide them with the opportunity to gain knowledge about hygiene and gain experience from modern technology.
Japan's Kusanoni Grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. The grant aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of factors, such as poverty and / or misfortune, that could directly threaten their lives, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote their self-reliance. Communities.
