Russian cats banned from international exhibition due to Ukraine crisis
PARIS (Reuters) - The International Cat Federation (FIFe) has banned all Russian cats from competing in exhibitions due to Russia's military operations in Ukraine. This is according to the press release from Sputnik on Thursday, March 3, 2022.
The FIFe board stressed that the decision was due to the shock and shock of Russia's attack on Ukraine, which left many innocent people dead and many others wounded. Hundreds of thousands were displaced from their homes.
The FIFe added that they will help organize donations to support cat breeders in Ukraine and will implement the ban on Russian cats until at least May 31, as it may be extended. Beyond that, depending on Russia's military operations in Ukraine.
FIFe is an international organization based in Paris, France and has 41 members from a total of 39 countries in Europe, South America and Asia. The organization is responsible for organizing events more than 700 times a year, and more than 200,000 cats from all over the world are featured in those events.
