(Phnom Penh): Orders and production of textiles, garments, footwear and bags are on the rise despite the global Kovid-19 crisis.
In 2021, orders for textiles, clothing, footwear and bags increased by more than 15 percent compared to 2019, but also saw a shortage of labor.
This was confirmed during a meeting between Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, and Mr. Oknha Kong Sang, President of the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia (GMAC) on the morning of March 3, 2022 at the Ministry of Labor.
Both sides raised the issue of labor shortages in the garment, footwear and bag making sectors and the government's solution.
Minister Ith Sam Heng said that through dialogue and listening to each other to jointly partnership between the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training with the Garment Manufacturers Association in Cambodia, which can solve further problems, in particular. In the context of high order growth, there is a shortage of manpower.
"The ministry will continue to urge the National Employment Agency (NEA) and private recruitment agencies to supply these labor-intensive factories to increase their production chains," he said.
According to the president of the Garment Manufacturers Association in Cambodia, the association currently has members in 462 garment factories, 13 shoe factories and 92 travel products.
