Royal Government warns of copying other people's work without permission
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has warned all art creators of plagiarism without permission because of its involvement in legal issues.
This was stated by Ms. Kittisangkhab Dr. Men Sam An, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection, representing Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia on the 24th National Cultural Day, March 3, 2022 at the Chaktomuk Conference Hall.
She said that in the past, rights holders in various countries have complained to Cambodia about cases of social media users in our country using foreign works to translate, edit or use without permission.
In Cambodia today, copying works from others is very common, especially song production. The perpetrators acted illegally by re-singing the original song (Cover) or using only the music to compose new lyrics or editing old songs without permission.
Cambodia is a State Party to the Bern Convention on the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works, in this sense, any natural person or legal entity that uses international works without permission is in violation of the law. As for the national work, Cambodia has a law on copyright and similar rights for protection.
After Cambodia becomes a full party to the Bern Convention, which will come into force on March 9, 2022, the Ministry of Culture calls on all writers, artists, entrepreneurs, companies, students to increase their ability to create new works. Do more on your own and avoid plagiarism.
Mr. Long Bonna Sirivath, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Culture, said that if you take work from others, you must have permission to avoid lawsuits and escape the law. The Ministry of Land Management requests the Capital and Provincial Governors to promote inspections and prevent the construction or demolition of unlicensed structures and to impose sanctions on the perpetrators.
