Cambodia Hosts 40th ASEAN Napol Conference on "Promoting Regional Harmony, Peace and Sustainability"
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of March 2, 2022, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, presided over the opening ceremony of the 40th ASEAN-Napol Summit under the theme "Promoting Harmonization, Peace and Sustainable Sustainability". .
The conference was held at Sokha Hotel with the participation of General Dr. Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner General, Chairman of the Police Delegation of ASEAN Member States and Dialogue Partners, Observers, Ambassadors of ASEAN Member States. In Cambodia, as well as some other ceremonies.
General Neth Savoeun emphasized that the 40th ASEAN-Napol Conference is a traditional annual gathering of police leaders of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) member states.
General Neth Savoeun said that the Cambodian police were assigned to host the 40th ASEAN Napoleonic Conference in 2020, but were missed for two years due to the spread of Kovid-19 disease. It is now a pleasure to host this conference in person as Cambodia and other countries reopen economic and social activities.
"The ASEAN-Napol Summit is important for linking and promoting partnerships and strengthening cooperation and mutual assistance, especially in the current context of global public health promotion," he said. Peace, stability, security and public order.
The General considered the presence of the ceremony on this occasion as a testament to the strong position of optimism in contributing to the search for strategies, methods and potential measures to continue to promote cooperation, peacekeeping, protection of interests and harmony. Congratulations to the people to ensure long-term social stability and security in the region.
Speaking on the occasion, Prince Sar Kheng called the organization of the 40th ASEAN Napole Conference in 2022 a new page for the police forces of ASEAN member countries, as the event takes place while the whole world speaks. Overall, ASEAN is striving to move away from the endangered Kovid-19 threat to a new normal path to prosperity and progress.
Britain says Russia can still send oil, gas because sanctions do not ban
LONDON (Reuters) - Russia will continue to ship oil and gas to Britain, despite a ban on Russian ships entering British ports, the British Ministry of Transport said on Wednesday. This was released by Reuters on Thursday, March 3, 2022.
Britain on Tuesday passed a law banning all Russian-linked ships from entering its ports as part of sanctions against Moscow over its attacks on Ukraine. This rule applies to ships owned by Russia, operated, operated, leased or registered with the Russian flag.
However, according to the British Ministry of Transport, the sanctions focus only on the ship itself, not on the cargo of the ship, so there is no ban on ships registered in other countries to transport Russian oil and gas to The United Kingdom.
