(Banteay Meanchey): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, presided over this morning the inauguration ceremony of the Poipet Cambodia-Thailand International Border Checkpoint Office in Banteay Meanchey Province. With Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kem Yan, Chairman of the Royal Government Working Group to visit Banteay Meanchey Province.
On that occasion, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng expressed his gratitude and appreciation for all the efforts of the relevant leaders who have actively paid attention to the Ministry of Interior, provincial leaders and other relevant units, as well as professional engineers for Joint efforts to overcome various difficulties to achieve the achievements celebrated today.
According to the report of General Seng Youan, Director General of the General Department of Logistics and Finance, the Poipet-Cambodia-Thailand International Border Crossing Inspection Office held a groundbreaking ceremony on the 30th of this month. October 2017 and completed on August 2, 2021 at a cost of 17 billion 13 million riels.
The general said that these buildings include 35.80 meters by 56.39 meters, 3 floors, equipped with electrical appliances, air conditioning, fans, vents, fire protection systems, security cameras, telephone systems, Internet, sound systems. Lightning protection system, 4 elevators and divided into two parts, the right side for departure passengers and the left side for arriving passengers.
(Sihanoukville): A story erupted a few days ago regarding the case of a Chinese man claiming that he was detained as a slave to sell blood in Sihanoukville, causing concern and distribution Facebook social network.
After receiving this information, the relevant law enforcement forces cooperated, researched and participated with the participation of the representative of the Chinese Embassy in Cambodia. But in the end, the truth was revealed by the above information, fabricated by only four Chinese individuals to escape legal responsibility after he entered Cambodia by mistake. Law.
After the authorities investigated and dismissed the case, Kouch Chamroeun, the governor of Sihanoukville, a province with a large Chinese population, both for tourism and investment, claimed that the fabrication of the crime of selling blood slaves brought pain. Heads for days can even affect the friendly relations between the two countries.
However, a group of Chinese people who fabricated this information were also arrested and the case was sent to court for legal action.
Please be reminded that the spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police on February 28, 2022 issued a statement clarifying the truth about the fabrication of the news that there was a case of a Chinese man being held as a slave to sell blood in the area of Chai Nat Town, Sihanoukville Province. Which affects the environment and social psychology, as well as confusion by many national and international public.
In a statement obtained by Fresh News, a spokesman for the National Police General Commission confirmed the following:
After receiving the above unfortunate information, the General Commission of the National Police directed the specialized unit to conduct an immediate investigation by inspecting various relevant locations and collecting the answers of some witnesses, and found that the above information was fabricated. Organized by LuYA YUAN LUN, a 31-year-old Chinese man (staying at Beeton Hospital, Cambodia-China, Phnom Penh) and 3 stakeholders, including:
The spokesman of the General Commission of the National Police calls on all media outlets and social media to revise the publication of the above fabricated information to avoid confusion from the national and international public.
At the same time, the General Commission of the National Police also requested that all journalists and users of social media should adhere to professional ethics by not disseminating information without clear sources, especially to avoid any conclusions. There is no clear evidence of selfishness, affecting the social environment and causing fear to national and international tourism and foreign investors in Cambodia.
