Japanese Government Provides Nearly $ 530,000 Grant for the Construction of Bridges in Kandal, Kratie and Irrigation Systems in Banteay Meanchey
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through its Embassy in Cambodia, has provided a grant of US $ 527,122 for the construction of bridges in Kandal and Kratie provinces and irrigation systems in Banteay Meanchey province under a grant scheme for the project. Small scale human security (Kusanoni grant).
The signing ceremony was presided over by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan and the three recipients at the Embassy of Japan on the afternoon of March 1, 2022.
* Second: Bridge construction project in Koulab commune, Chit Borey district, Kratie province (budget $ 136,791) Recipient: Department of Rural Development, Kratie province.
In this project, the grant money will be used to rebuild a bridge where the bridge supports on both sides were badly damaged by rain and termites. If the bridge collapses, it will impede the daily activities of the people in the project area.
In addition, the bridge is currently flooded about five times a year, and it is difficult to cross the bridge back to the district, which impedes the transportation of agricultural products to markets and daily activities such as going to school. And hospitals. It is expected that the project will improve the situation and that more than 3,000 people will have access to public facilities throughout the year.
* Third: Rehabilitation project in Banteay Meanchey province (budget of 266,496 USD) Recipient: Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, Banteay Meanchey province.
In this project, the grant will be used to restore dams and build three sluices. The irrigation system was built in the 1970s and was renovated by the aid agency in 2006.
Mr. Mikami Masahiro stated that recently observed that Cambodia has been developing rapidly. However, he acknowledged that there are still some challenges in improving the quality of life of the people.
The Japanese government has provided yen financing for large-scale infrastructure projects such as the development of the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port and the improvement of National Road 5, as well as to support the emergency response to the 19-Kovid crisis with an additional 20 billion. Yen.
The ambassador stressed that such large-scale support is likely to attract a lot of media attention, but the Japanese government considers it equally important to provide support in rural areas and work. Stay close to local people through grants for Japanese NGOs and Kusanoni grants.
It should be noted that the Japanese Kusanoni grant started in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level.
The grant aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of factors, such as: poverty and / or misfortune that could directly threaten their lives, livelihoods and dignity, as well as: The self of the community.
Over the past 30 years, the Japanese government has provided more than $ 65 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 668 Kusano projects across Cambodia.
