BANGKOK: Thailand and Malaysia have agreed to increase bilateral trade to $ 30 billion, the Bangkok Post reported.
Prayut said the first official visit by a foreign leader to Thailand since the outbreak of Kovid-19 shows the close ties between the two countries.
The two leaders reached agreements in a number of key areas.
The Bangkok Post reports that one of these areas is border reconnection, with the two countries continuing to reopen the border for full vaccination without the need for a vaccination.
A joint working group will be set up to set guidelines for this, as construction projects along the border will soon reopen to boost trade, investment and tourism.
With the volume of trade and cross-border trade increasing during the epidemic, the two countries have agreed to strive for bilateral trade to reach $ 30 billion by 2025.
The two countries will also seek new opportunities towards sustainable economies, including the digital economy and the BCG green economy model. According to the Bangkok Post.
