He said that for 2022, the real estate sector is projected to grow at 4.1 percent after a two-year decline in 2020 to -6.2 percent and 2021 to 0.6 percent. . Phnom Penh, Kandal and Kep become the first anti-personnel landmine-free zones as four more provinces finish this year
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Heng Ratana, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC), said that Kandal, Kep and Phnom Penh have ended the problem of areas affected by all anti-personnel mines first.
Mr. Heng Ratana confirmed that CMAC has completed the task of completing the operation to clear the area affected by anti-personnel landmines, which is known and has a polygonal record in the national data in Kep, Kandal and Phnom Penh.
Kandal province has 3 polygons (mine plantations) with an area of 64 hectares, Kep province has 8 polygons equal to 69.8 hectares and Phnom Penh has 14 polygons equal to 125.7 hectares. Thus, the two provinces of Kandal and Kep, as well as Phnom Penh, are the first provinces / capitals to be threatened by landmines in the Kingdom of Cambodia.
The next target is the mobile CMAC unit entering Sihanoukville, Kampot, Kampong Cham and Takeo provinces, which aims to end the problem of anti-personnel landmines in 2022.
Please note that for Kep province, CMAC has received assistance from partners through the Mine Authority, NPA, and other provinces have received assistance from the Chinese government.
In fact, there may continue to be problems with unexploded ordnance from other wars, such as cluster munitions, dropped bombs, and unexploded ordnance other than anti-personnel mines.
As the issue of anti-personnel mines is under the Ottawa International Convention, which has 164 signatories, of which 133 are member states, including the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Mr. Heng Ratana further stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia, as an active member state, has committed to fully implement this Convention in accordance with the Cambodia Mine Action Strategic Plan by the end of 2025. Therefore, the main goal is to focus on solving the problem of anti-personnel mines by 2025.
The Kingdom of Cambodia has suffered from the longest and most severe war in the world, so the remnants of war weapons are also very large. Therefore, it is not possible to solve the problem of all kinds of munitions by 2025.
Based on the Royal Government's plan, CMAC has launched a four-year strategic plan (2022-2025) to clear more than 700 square kilometers of land affected by anti-personnel landmines. The remaining 800 square kilometers are about 90% of the remaining landmines.
"Please avoid the misconception that a landmine-free province is free of other munitions, in fact, a landmine-free province has not yet been depleted of munitions from other wars," he said. "Such as bombs dropped from planes, cluster munitions and all kinds of unexploded ordnance, so please be careful and continue to take action to clear all those unexploded ordnance from the Kingdom."
